1. Visitor (not signed in)
Opens the public website (English or Hindi, switchable).
Browses Services (8 groups, 81 service pages), About, the tender flow chart, and the Privacy, Terms and Disclaimer pages.
Sends an enquiry from a service page, or uses the WhatsApp or Call button. The enquiry needs consent and a valid email.
Asks for a portal login on the Request Access page.
Waits for staff to create an account. Visitors can't sign themselves up.

2. Subscriber (client user)
Gets an invite email and opens the link, which is valid for 72 hours.
Sets a password of at least 10 characters with a letter and a digit.
Signs in at /login. Five wrong passwords lock the account for 15 minutes.
Lands on the Dashboard: open tenders by department, state and value, plus a closing-soon list.
Search tenders by keyword with filters, then open a tender to see its full details.
They can save a search, add the tender to the Watchlist, or export to Excel.
Watchlist: keep private notes and get deadline reminders by email.
Alerts: create alert profiles (categories, keywords, states, value band, frequency).
They can preview a digest, send themselves a test email, pause a profile, and see tenders that were held back.
Results: look up past tender results and winners, and use the rate analysis and bid-band guidance.
Desk: click "New request" on a tender and track the case.
They can see the stage, checklists, pre-bid queries, submission, result and shared documents, and upload documents in client slots.
They never see internal strategy or notes.
Reports: download the weekly report as PDF or Excel, but only after staff have released it.
Profile / Help: view their own details, FAQs and contact options. Then sign out.

3. Account Admin (client administrator, tenant_admin)
Can do everything a Subscriber can, plus:
Open Profile and click Invite to add a person as Subscriber or Account Admin.
Each organization has at most 3 active logins by default.
Promote or demote members, or deactivate them.

4. Analyst (staff)
Signs in and sees the same portal as clients, plus the admin menu Access Requests and Enquiries.
Reads website enquiries and access requests, and moves them through New → Contacted → Qualified → Closed.
Opens Desk, which is the staff view. It shows open cases by stage, deadline risk, unassigned cases and follow-ups due.
Opens a case and works through it:
Moves it through the 9 stages, from request received to follow-up.
Writes the summary shared with the client.
Ticks eligibility and technical checklists.
Raises pre-bid queries and records the department's responses.
Records the submission (online with a portal reference, or offline with courier details).
Records the result, the EMD and the follow-ups.
Uploads documents, marked internal or shared.
Writes internal strategy and notes, attaches a rate analysis, and can mark a case cancelled or withdrawn.

5. Case Manager (staff)
Can do everything an Analyst can, plus:
Assign a case to a staff owner.
Reopen a closed case.
Open Weekly Reports, preview each client's bundle, and release it to one client or to a whole plan. Releases are logged and can't be edited later.
Gets an email whenever a client raises a new request, and reminders about unowned cases.

6. Platform Admin (staff, full control)
Can do everything a Case Manager can, plus:
Organizations: create a client with a plan and first admin, edit it, suspend it, and invite, deactivate or reset passwords for its members.
Plans: edit alert limits, profile limits and login limits.
Masters: edit states, cities, departments and categories, and resolve unmapped names from the feed.
Ingestion: run or back-fill the tender data feed, and upload tenders from Excel.
Mail queue: check email status, and send, retry or cancel messages.
Jobs: see schedules and results, and run a job on demand.
Weekly Reports: generate the bundles.
Audit Log: read the record of sign-ins, admin changes, releases and case changes.
